# MITRE ATT&CK Mapping Notes

## Overview

MITRE ATT&CK is a knowledge base of adversary tactics and techniques based on real-world observations.

It helps security teams:

* Understand attacker behavior
* Classify threats
* Improve detection capabilities
* Standardize investigations

---

# Activities Performed During Project

The simulated activities were mapped to relevant MITRE ATT&CK techniques.

---

## Network Reconnaissance

### Activity

Nmap Port Scanning

### Command

```bash
nmap -sS -A <TARGET-IP>
```

### MITRE Mapping

| Technique ID | Technique Name           |
| ------------ | ------------------------ |
| T1046        | Network Service Scanning |

### Why It Matches

The attacker attempts to identify available services and open ports before exploitation.

---

## Authentication Failure Simulation

### Activity

Multiple failed login attempts.

### MITRE Mapping

| Technique ID | Technique Name |
| ------------ | -------------- |
| T1110        | Brute Force    |

### Why It Matches

Repeated authentication attempts are commonly associated with password guessing attacks.

---

## PowerShell Execution

### Activity

Executed PowerShell commands.

### Commands

```powershell
powershell -ep bypass
whoami
ipconfig
net user
```

### MITRE Mapping

| Technique ID | Technique Name |
| ------------ | -------------- |
| T1059.001    | PowerShell     |

### Why It Matches

PowerShell is frequently used for execution, enumeration, and automation during attacks.

---

## Scheduled Task Creation

### Activity

Persistence simulation.

### Command

```powershell
schtasks /create /sc onlogon /tn "Updater" /tr "cmd.exe"
```

### MITRE Mapping

| Technique ID | Technique Name |
| ------------ | -------------- |
| T1053.005    | Scheduled Task |

### Why It Matches

Scheduled tasks can provide persistence across system restarts.

---

## File Modification Activity

### Activity

Created and modified files.

### Commands

```bash
touch ~/testfile
echo "malwaretest" >> ~/testfile
```

### MITRE Mapping

| Technique ID | Technique Name                               |
| ------------ | -------------------------------------------- |
| T1070        | Indicator Removal on Host (Related Activity) |

### Why It Matches

File modifications often occur during malware deployment, persistence, or attacker activity.

